/-!
# Algebraic Kummer normal form for tame trait invariants

Source: ILO XVI 3.7, p.254; Stacks Tags 09I3, 0325, 09EB and 09ER.
(blueprint: lem-per-tame-trait-invariant-kummer-presentation)
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

open Polynomial

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Tame invariants are a strictly henselian DVR and admit a uniformizer Kummer
presentation of degree dividing the possibly nonfaithful acting group's order.

Source: ILO XVI 3.7, p.254; Stacks Tags 09I3, 0325, 09EB and 09ER.
(blueprint: lem-per-tame-trait-invariant-kummer-presentation).

Proof: Put A = B^H and extend the action to L = Frac B. For an invariant fraction
b/c, multiply its denominator by all conjugates of c: their nonzero product d is
in A, and (b/c)d belongs to B and is invariant. Thus Frac A is L^H. Replace H by
its faithful image G in Aut(L); fixed-field theory gives the finite Galois degree
n = |G|, positive and dividing |H|. Therefore n is a unit in R and hence in A.
Restrict the normalized integer valuation of B to Frac A. Its value subgroup
contains a positive value, namely that of a uniformizer of R: finite integrality
and locality make this uniformizer a nonunit in B, and injectivity makes it
nonzero. Let e be the least positive value. The nonnegative-value elements of
Frac A are exactly A, by the invariant-fraction description. In each nonzero
ideal choose an element of least value; division shows that it generates the
ideal. The positive-value elements form the maximal ideal, generated by an
element of value e. Hence A is a DVR. B is its integral closure in L: a root of
a monic A-polynomial cannot have negative value, since its leading term would
then have uniquely least value. A is finite over R as a submodule of finite B;
the complete-local product theorem for finite algebras makes the domain A
complete local, and completeness gives henselianity.
The local finite map R -> B induces a finite extension of residue fields.
Since the residue field of R is separably closed this extension is purely
inseparable. H acts trivially on it, because Frobenius is injective. Averaging
any lift in B by |H| inverse produces an invariant lift with the same residue.
Thus k_A -> k_B is bijective. In particular k_A is separably closed, so A is
strictly henselian. The Galois DVR degree formula now gives n = e: B has one
maximal ideal and residue degree one. For uniformizers pi of A and t of B,
pi = u*t^n with u a unit. In the separably closed residue field X^n-u has a
nonzero simple root, since n is a unit. Hensel lifting gives a unit w with
w^n=u. Then tau=w*t is a uniformizer and tau^n=pi. The totally ramified
uniformizer-presentation theorem gives generation and a monic minimal
polynomial of degree n. The monic polynomial X^n-pi also annihilates tau and
has degree n, so it is that polynomial. Its evaluation equivalence is precisely
the displayed equivalence. All constructions allow n=1 and action kernels. -/
theorem tame_trait_invariant_kummer_presentation
    (R B : Type u) [CommRing R] [CommRing B] [IsDomain R] [IsDomain B]
    [IsDiscreteValuationRing R] [IsDiscreteValuationRing B]
    [IsStrictlyHenselianLocalRing R] [IsStrictlyHenselianLocalRing B]
    [IsAdicComplete (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R) R]
    [IsAdicComplete (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal B) B]
    [CharZero (FractionRing R)] [CharZero (FractionRing B)]
    (hRchar : ringChar (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R) ≠ 0)
    (hBchar : ringChar (IsLocalRing.ResidueField B) ≠ 0)
    [Algebra R B] [Module.Finite R B]
    (hinj : Function.Injective (algebraMap R B))
    (H : Type v) [Group H] [Finite H] [MulSemiringAction H B] [SMulCommClass H R B]
    (horder : IsUnit (Nat.card H : R)) :
    IsDiscreteValuationRing (FixedPoints.subalgebra R B H) ∧
    IsStrictlyHenselianLocalRing (FixedPoints.subalgebra R B H) ∧
    ∃ n : ℕ, 0 < n ∧ n ∣ Nat.card H ∧
      @Module.finrank (FractionRing (FixedPoints.subalgebra R B H)) (FractionRing B) _ _
        (FractionRing.liftAlgebra (FixedPoints.subalgebra R B H) (FractionRing B)).toModule
          = n ∧
      IsUnit (n : FixedPoints.subalgebra R B H) ∧
      ∃ (pi : FixedPoints.subalgebra R B H) (tau : B),
        Irreducible pi ∧ Irreducible tau ∧
        tau ^ n = algebraMap (FixedPoints.subalgebra R B H) B pi ∧
        ∃ e : ((FixedPoints.subalgebra R B H)[X] ⧸ Ideal.span {X ^ n - C pi})
            ≃ₐ[FixedPoints.subalgebra R B H] B,
          ∀ Q : (FixedPoints.subalgebra R B H)[X],
            e (Ideal.Quotient.mk _ Q) = aeval tau Q := by sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
